# docs/ADMIN.md §1.2, §2.9: the root helper of !new-workshop (zab.admin.confighelper). Started
# by zab-admin-config.path, it reads and deletes the bot's request, validates the new entry and
# the whole new admin.toml with the strict loader, then writes the new bot's key (root:zab-admin
# 0640, never over a file) and admin.toml (atomically, keeping admin.toml.bak). Its outcome, and
# a record of each change the bot can't alter, stay in its own state directory. It restarts
# nothing: the bot that asked restarts itself once its apply has posted its result.
# Root, with two capabilities: CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE to read and delete the request in zab-admin's
# 0700 directory, CAP_CHOWN to give the key its group. No network.
# What root runs (§1.6, #437): the system interpreter the release's venv is built from
# (deploy.sh's PYTHON), not the venv's: zab-build builds the venv while third-party code runs,
# and its pyvenv.cfg would choose the stdlib. Nothing of the venv runs as root. -I -B -S: no
# environment, no site processing (no .pth line), no bytecode written; sys.path is the standard
# library and the release's src/, resolved once through `current`. The helper imports
# confighelper, config and checks, and the standard library (tests/admin/test_admin_imports.py,
# HELPER_IMPORTS).
# What it writes (#437): ProtectSystem=strict makes everything read-only but these.
# - /etc/zab-admin, the directory: admin.toml is replaced by a rename there, which needs its
#   directory writable (admin.toml.new; admin.toml.bak.new, a link to the old file; then both
#   renames). A read-only mount on admin.toml would make the rename fail.
# - /etc/zab-admin/secrets/bots: the new bot's key, created with O_EXCL. The rest of secrets/
#   is hidden, under an empty read-only tmpfs.
# - /var/lib/zab-admin-config, root's: its outcome, its record, and requests/, the bot's (it
#   deletes the request there): no path the bot can swap is named here (review of #421).
# Every other file deploy.sh installs in /etc/zab-admin is a read-only mount: the trust files,
# the break-glass keys, and the nftables files root loads, which keep the console, the waker
# and Caddy off the role. Each without "-": a missing one would be one the helper could create,
# so the unit doesn't start instead. A deploy test checks this list against what a bootstrap
# installs there. What stays open: the other bots' keys in secrets/bots (systemd names only
# paths that exist when the unit starts), and any file an operator leaves in /etc/zab-admin by
# hand, such as a rollback copy of admin.toml: nothing the helper can't do through admin.toml.
# /var/lib/zab-admin (the fleet key, the bot's state, the participants' keys) is hidden.
[Unit]
Description=zab admin config helper: add a !new-workshop entry to admin.toml

[Service]
Type=oneshot
ExecStart=/usr/bin/python3 -I -B -S -c "import os, sys; sys.path.append(os.path.realpath('/opt/zab-admin/current/src')); from zab.admin.confighelper import main; sys.exit(main())"
ReadWritePaths=/etc/zab-admin /var/lib/zab-admin-config
ReadOnlyPaths=/etc/zab-admin/allowed_signers /etc/zab-admin/backup_recipients /etc/zab-admin/break-glass
ReadOnlyPaths=/etc/zab-admin/imds.nft /etc/zab-admin/nftables-imds.nft /etc/zab-admin/nftables-stop-imds.nft
TemporaryFileSystem=/etc/zab-admin/secrets:ro
BindPaths=/etc/zab-admin/secrets/bots
InaccessiblePaths=-/var/lib/zab-admin
TimeoutStartSec=60
UMask=0077
NoNewPrivileges=true
PrivateTmp=true
PrivateDevices=true
PrivateNetwork=true
IPAddressDeny=any
ProtectSystem=strict
ProtectHome=true
ProtectProc=invisible
ProcSubset=pid
ProtectClock=true
ProtectHostname=true
ProtectKernelLogs=true
ProtectKernelModules=true
ProtectKernelTunables=true
ProtectControlGroups=true
RestrictNamespaces=true
RestrictRealtime=true
RestrictSUIDSGID=true
RestrictAddressFamilies=AF_UNIX
LockPersonality=true
MemoryDenyWriteExecute=true
SystemCallArchitectures=native
SystemCallFilter=@system-service
CapabilityBoundingSet=CAP_CHOWN CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE
